// A coffee machine: behaviour in temporal Clafer, in three modeling styles.
//
// A model with temporal operators is dynamic: its instances are traces, sequences of
// states that change over time. Press Next to see a trace. It ends with a loop back to
// an earlier state, and repeats from there forever. Alloy only.
//
// The styles can be mixed freely, and each one fits a different kind of statement:
//   1. transitions: how the machine moves from state to state
//   2. temporal logic: exact rules about the next step
//   3. Dwyer's patterns: requirements, read as plain sentences

// Two kinds of clafers change differently. A final clafer does not change during a trace,
// so milk is an option of the product: another trace may have the other variant.
final milk ?

// Other clafers come and go between states. coin is an event from the outside:
// it is present in the states in which a customer inserts a coin.
coin ?

xor machine
    // 1. TRANSITIONS
    //
    // initial: the state in which the trace starts. A transition holds whenever its
    // source does, and comes in four forms:
    //
    //   a --> b        next step: from a, the next state is b
    //   a -->> b       multi-step: a lasts until b, however long that takes
    //   a -[g]-> b     guarded next step: only when the guard g holds
    //   a -[g]->> b    guarded multi-step

    // guarded, next step: a coin starts the machine
    initial idle -[ coin ]-> heating

    // guarded, next step: heating is done at the required temperature
    heating -[ temperature = 10 ]-> brewing

    // guarded, multi-step: only with milk is the coffee frothed
    // a state with two ways out: one on the clafer, the other as a constraint
    brewing -[ milk ]->> frothing
    [ brewing -[ no milk ]->> ready ]

    // unguarded, multi-step: frothing takes as long as it takes
    frothing -->> ready

    // unguarded, next step
    ready --> idle

    // 2. TEMPORAL LOGIC
    //
    // X (next), F (eventually), G (globally), U (until). A constraint holds in every state
    // by default, so there is no need to start it with G.
    //
    // A transition says only what happens when it fires: without these constraints, the
    // machine could leave idle with no coin at all, or heating before it is hot.
    [ idle && no coin => X idle ]
    [ heating && temperature < 10 => X heating ]

    // 3. DWYER'S PATTERNS
    //
    // Patterns say what must hold in a part of the trace, without any operators. They are
    // sugar for temporal logic: <pattern> [before p | after p | between p and q].

    // absence: a coin is not accepted once the machine has started
    [ never coin between heating and idle ]

    // response: every coin is eventually served
    [ ready must follow coin ]

// A value can change between states too: the temperature of the water, from 0 to 10.
// Everything about it is in its own context, so this is the value here. The machine reads
// the temperature, and the temperature follows the machine.
temperature -> integer
    // the machine starts cold: initially anchors a constraint to the start of the trace
    [ initially (this = 0) ]

    // transitions work on values too: heating adds 5
    [ this = 0 -[ machine.heating ]-> this = 5 ]
    [ this = 5 -[ machine.heating ]-> this = 10 ]

    // and idle takes 5 away, down to 0
    [ this = 10 -[ machine.idle ]-> this = 5 ]
    [ this = 5 -[ machine.idle ]-> this = 0 ]
    [ this = 0 -[ machine.idle ]-> this = 0 ]

    // no transition is needed here: it holds in each of these states
    [ machine.brewing || machine.frothing || machine.ready => this = 10 ]

    // and the machine is still hot in the step after ready
    [ this = 10 -[ machine.ready ]-> this = 10 ]

    // An assertion must hold in all traces. Press Check to check it: a failed one gives a
    // counterexample, a trace in which it does not hold.
    assert [ this >= 0 && this <= 10 ]

// A scenario: one concrete trace to try, written as a chain of transitions. A chain is a
// sequence, read from left to right: the three states come one after the other. It is
// anchored with initially, so it holds once, at the start of the trace. Comment it out to
// see other traces, and change it to see if the model allows a behaviour: a scenario that
// contradicts the model has no traces, and an unsat core shows why.
[ initially (machine.idle --> machine.idle --> machine.heating) ]
